# Source files are UTF-8 with NO byte order mark. .editorconfig (charset = utf-8)
# stops editors creating them; this file and the CI check below catch what slips past.
#
# MEASURED (git 2.40.0.windows.1) — `working-tree-encoding=UTF-8` does NOT strip a
# BOM, and neither do the spellings UTF8, utf-8 or CP65001. Git's internal encoding
# is already UTF-8, so it short-circuits the conversion and the BOM lands in the
# index byte-for-byte. Do not re-propose it: it reads like enforcement and is a no-op.
#
# A clean filter does work, and is what the rule below declares. It is OPT-IN by
# nature — an undefined filter driver is silently ignored, so a fresh clone has no
# protection until someone runs the one-time config:
#
#     git config filter.nobom.clean "perl -0777 -pe 's/^\xef\xbb\xbf//'"
#     git config filter.nobom.required true
#
# perl rather than sed because `sed '1s/^\xEF\xBB\xBF//'` is GNU-only: BSD sed on
# macOS reads \x as a literal 'x', never matches, and strips nothing without error.
# `required = true` matters for the same reason — without it a missing or failing
# filter passes the BOM through silently instead of failing the add.
#
# Cost is roughly 40ms per file re-hashed on Windows (process spawn): invisible on an
# ordinary `git add`, seconds on a full-tree re-add. That cost, plus the fresh-clone
# gap, is why this is a convenience and NOT the guarantee. The actual guard is the
# "Check for UTF-8 BOMs" step in .github/workflows/ci.yml, which needs no local setup.
*.cs filter=nobom
